<!--
    VINTF framework manifest for the recovery ramdisk.

    WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
    ====================
    system/hwservicemanager/service.cpp:150 refuses to run when
    android.hidl.manager@1.2::IServiceManager/default does not resolve to a
    transport in a VINTF manifest:

        auto transport = android::hardware::getTransport(ServiceManager::descriptor, serviceName);
        if (transport == android::vintf::Transport::EMPTY) {
            ALOGI("HIDL is not supported on this device so hwservicemanager is not needed");
            property_set("hwservicemanager.disabled", "true");
            while (true) { ... sleep(10); }
        }

    getTransport (system/hwservicemanager/Vintf.cpp:40-71) looks the name up in
    the framework manifest first and in the device manifest second and returns
    EMPTY when neither declares it.  A stock device never reaches that branch
    because /system/etc/vintf/manifest.xml ships the entry below, but a TWRP
    recovery ramdisk contains no generated framework manifest at all.  Measured
    consequence on this device before this file existed:
        getprop hwservicemanager.disabled   ->  true
        getprop hwservicemanager.ready      ->  (unset)
    so the action in init.recovery.qcom.rc that starts the keymaster and
    gatekeeper HALs could never fire, and Decrypt_Data() had no keymaster to
    unwrap the metadata key with.

    A second, independent consequence of the same gap: registering a HIDL service
    is gated on its own descriptor being declared, and that gate is unconditional
    in this build (system/libhidl/transport/ServiceManagement.cpp:988-1001):

        if (kEnforceVintfManifest && !isTrebleTestingOverride()) { ...
            if (transport != Transport::HWBINDER) { "must be in VINTF manifest in
            order to register/get."; return UNKNOWN_ERROR; } }

    and kEnforceVintfManifest is true because build/make/core/config.mk:785 sets
    PRODUCT_ENFORCE_VINTF_MANIFEST := true as a .KATI_READONLY value.  Without the
    keymaster and gatekeeper entries below both HALs would start and then fail to
    register, which is just as fatal to metadata decryption.

    WHY ONE FILE AND NOT TWO
    ========================
    A real device splits this over /system/etc/vintf/manifest.xml (framework,
    declaring android.hidl.*) and /vendor/etc/vintf/manifest.xml (device,
    declaring the vendor HALs).  The stock vendor manifest declares exactly
    keymaster 4.0 and gatekeeper 1.0, at stock/vendor/etc/vintf/manifest.xml:87
    and :77.  In recovery that split is a liability: hwservicemanager reads the
    manifests once and caches them (system/libvintf/VintfObject.cpp, Get()), while
    the device-side path would sit under /vendor, which TWRP mounts and unmounts
    underneath it.  /system is the recovery ramdisk itself and is never shadowed,
    and the framework manifest is consulted first (Vintf.cpp:59 precedes :64), so
    every entry is declared here.

    Declaring vendor HALs in a type="framework" manifest is safe on this code
    path: the only two places that branch on the manifest type are
    HalManifest::sepolicyVersion() (CHECKs DEVICE) and HalManifest::vendorNdks()
    (CHECKs FRAMEWORK), and neither is reachable from getTransport(), which only
    iterates instances (system/libvintf/HalManifest.cpp:285-301).

    VERSION CHOICE
    ==============
    android.hidl.manager is declared as 1.2, not 1.0.  ServiceManager::descriptor
    comes from the generated interface the service implements
    (system/hwservicemanager/ServiceManager.h includes
    android/hidl/manager/1.2/IServiceManager.h), so the lookup is for
    android.hidl.manager@1.2::IServiceManager, and a declared version only matches
    if it is at least the queried one
    (system/libvintf/include/vintf/Version.h:61-67):

        Version(2, 1).minorAtLeast(Version(2, 2)) == false

    Declaring 1.0 would therefore NOT satisfy it and hwservicemanager would keep
    disabling itself.  The four entries are copied from the sources the platform
    itself ships for this purpose:

      system/hwservicemanager/hwservicemanager_no_max.xml
        android.hidl.manager 1.2 and android.hidl.token 1.0.  The _no_max variant
        is used on purpose: the sibling hwservicemanager.xml adds max-level="8", a
        frozen-API qualification for updateable framework modules that has no
        meaning for a recovery ramdisk.
      stock/vendor/etc/vintf/manifest.xml:76-94
        keymaster 4.0 and gatekeeper 1.0, verbatim.

    WHERE IT LANDS
    ==============
    recovery/root/system/etc/vintf/manifest.xml becomes
    /system/etc/vintf/manifest.xml in the recovery ramdisk
    (build/make/core/Makefile:2817-2818 copies recovery/root into
    $(TARGET_RECOVERY_OUT)).

    Do NOT move this into /system/etc/vintf/manifest/ instead: that fragment
    directory is only read when the main manifest parsed successfully
    (system/libvintf/VintfObject.cpp:454-461 guards addDirectoryManifests on
    fetchOneHalManifest(kSystemManifest) returning OK).  The pre-existing
    android.system.keystore2-service.xml fragment in that directory is inert for
    exactly that reason.

    NOTE FOR EDITORS: XML forbids two consecutive hyphens inside a comment, which
    is why the section rules above are made of "=" and not of dashes; please keep
    it that way.  Being precise about the consequence, because it is easy to
    overstate: libvintf parses with tinyxml2 (system/libvintf/parse_xml.cpp:32)
    and tolerant of the sequence, and assemble_vintf accepts such a file, so a
    dash rule here would NOT have taken the HIDL bring-up down.  It is a
    well-formedness defect, not a device-blocking one; but expat-based tooling
    (python xml.etree, xmllint) rejects the document outright, so the regression
    check keeps it out anyway.

    Regression check: tools/verify_decrypt_prereqs.mjs asserts this file exists,
    is well-formed, and carries all four entries.  The strongest check available
    off-device is the platform's own parser:
        out/host/linux-x86/bin/assemble_vintf -i <this file> -o /tmp/out.xml
    which exits 0 here and normalises the four entries to
        android.hidl.manager@1.2::IServiceManager/default
        android.hidl.token@1.0::ITokenManager/default
        android.hardware.keymaster@4.0::IKeymasterDevice/default
        android.hardware.gatekeeper@1.0::IGatekeeper/default
    all on hwbinder.
-->
<manifest version="1.0" type="framework">
    <hal format="hidl">
        <name>android.hidl.manager</name>
        <transport>hwbinder</transport>
        <version>1.2</version>
        <interface>
            <name>IServiceManager</name>
            <instance>default</instance>
        </interface>
    </hal>
    <hal format="hidl">
        <name>android.hidl.token</name>
        <transport>hwbinder</transport>
        <version>1.0</version>
        <interface>
            <name>ITokenManager</name>
            <instance>default</instance>
        </interface>
    </hal>
    <hal format="hidl">
        <name>android.hardware.keymaster</name>
        <transport>hwbinder</transport>
        <version>4.0</version>
        <interface>
            <name>IKeymasterDevice</name>
            <instance>default</instance>
        </interface>
    </hal>
    <hal format="hidl">
        <name>android.hardware.gatekeeper</name>
        <transport>hwbinder</transport>
        <version>1.0</version>
        <interface>
            <name>IGatekeeper</name>
            <instance>default</instance>
        </interface>
    </hal>
</manifest>
